# Fixture PDFs are NOT committed by default.
#
# A real invoice carries a company name, a postal address, an email and a set of
# amounts. Committing one puts all of that in git history permanently, where
# rewriting it later is a force-push across every clone. That should be a
# deliberate decision someone makes per document, not the default this directory
# hands them.
#
# The goldens (*.expected.json) ARE committed: they are what makes a run
# reproducible and reviewable, and they contain only what you chose to write in
# them. dataset.py fails with a readable message when a golden is here but its PDF
# is not, so a fresh clone is told what is missing rather than quietly scoring less.
#
# To commit one anyway:  git add -f evals/fixtures/that-one.pdf

*.pdf

# Extraction results, keyed by pdf + model + prompt + schema. Regenerable, and
# they hold the same invoice data the PDFs do.
.cache/
